{"name":"Fluorescent Red","hex":"#ff5555","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fluorescent","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to fluorescence.","Exhibiting or produced by fluorescence."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fluorescent light."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fluorescent"},{"term":"red","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of the setting sun, blood, and strawberries; the color which is evoked by the longest visible wavelengths (between about 625–740 nm), and a primary additive color.","A revolutionary socialist or (most commonly) a Communist;  a Bolshevik, a supporter of the Bolsheviks in the Russian Civil War."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a red hue.","Having an orange-brown or orange-blond colour; ginger."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fluorescence","blocks":[{"text":"Fluorescence is one of two kinds of photoluminescence, the emission of light by a substance that has absorbed light or other electromagnetic radiation. When exposed to ultraviolet radiation, many substances will glow (fluoresce) with colored visible light. The color of the light emitted depends on the chemical composition of the substance. Fluorescent materials generally cease to glow nearly immediately when the radiation source stops. This distinguishes them from the other type of light emission, phosphorescence. Phosphorescent materials continue to emit light for some time after the radiation stops.\nThis difference in duration is a result of quantum spin effects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Fluorescent_minerals_hg.jpg/330px-Fluorescent_minerals_hg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fluorescence"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg/330px-Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Adaptive-Evolution-of-Eel-Fluorescent-Proteins-from-Fatty-Acid-Binding-Proteins-Produces-Bright-pone.0140972.g001.jpg/500px-Adaptive-Evolution-of-Eel-Fluorescent-Proteins-from-Fatty-Acid-Binding-Proteins-Produces-Bright-pone.0140972.g001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Adaptive-Evolution-of-Eel-Fluorescent-Proteins-from-Fatty-Acid-Binding-Proteins-Produces-Bright-pone.0140972.g001.jpg/500px-Adaptive-Evolution-of-Eel-Fluorescent-Proteins-from-Fatty-Acid-Binding-Proteins-Produces-Bright-pone.0140972.g001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fluorescent marine organisms","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAdaptive-Evolution-of-Eel-Fluorescent-Proteins-from-Fatty-Acid-Binding-Proteins-Produces-Bright-pone.0140972.g001.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Black_light_theatre_Prague_HILT_13.jpg/500px-Black_light_theatre_Prague_HILT_13.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Black_light_theatre_Prague_HILT_13.jpg/1280px-Black_light_theatre_Prague_HILT_13.jp